{{ macros.section(3, "CMK Virtual Machine", id="cmk-virtual-machine") }}


{{ macros.non_core('virtual-machine.cmk', kind='module', detail='It runs supervised, so it needs a `cmk run` / `mk.interpret` invocation, and it builds on the core [runtime](@/runtime) continuation/trampoline substrate.', mkdocs=mkdocs) }}

The goals **after** the current target on the command line are the *continuation* (what is left to do).
The `virtual-machine.cmk` module exposes that continuation[^cont] as a small **CEK machine** bound to the live make
process:

* **C**: the current goal (the pending goals, read from the command line),
* **E**: an environment of bindings,
* **K**: a tree of saved `{continuation, environment}` frames.

{% include "includes/cek-anim.md" %}


Every transfer re-dispatches **flat** through the supervisor's trampoline, so even an unbounded
`goto`-loop runs at constant process depth (no nesting).

```make
$(call include.plugins, virtual-machine.cmk)
```

#### Operators
<hr class="section-rule lvl-4">

Use these **inline in a recipe**, where the current target is the goal:

<table class="docutils">
<thead><tr><th>operator</th><th>C/E/K</th><th>effect</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>${__vm__.peek}</code> / <code>.rest</code> / <code>.depth</code></td><td>C read</td><td>inspect pending goals (top / below-top / count)</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>$(call vm.push, &lt;g&gt;)</code></td><td>C xfer</td><td>run <code>&lt;g&gt;</code> next, then resume (subroutine, implicit return)</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>$(call vm.drop)</code></td><td>C xfer</td><td>skip the next pending goal</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>$(call vm.goto, &lt;g&gt;)</code></td><td>C xfer</td><td>tail-jump to <code>&lt;g&gt;</code>, keeping the goals after it</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>$(call vm.replace, &lt;gs&gt;)</code></td><td>C xfer</td><td>replace the whole continuation</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>$(call __vm__.getenv, &lt;k&gt;)</code> / <code>$(call __vm__.setenv, &lt;k&gt;, &lt;v&gt;)</code></td><td>E</td><td>read / bind one env key</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>$(call vm.call, &lt;g&gt;)</code> / <code>$(call vm.return)</code></td><td>K xfer</td><td>call saves <code>{goal,cont,env}</code> + jumps; return pops + resumes</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>$(call vm.yield, &lt;v&gt;)</code> (= <code>vm.ctx.send</code>)</td><td>K xfer</td><td>generator yield: bind <code>&lt;v&gt;</code> as <code>yielded</code> in the caller env, then return</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>$(call vm.fork, "&lt;a b&gt;")</code></td><td>K</td><td>record a branching frame and run the branches via <code>flux.pipeline</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>${make} __vm__.backtrace</code></td><td>K read</td><td>print the full control tree (K frames + live continuation)</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>${__vm__}</code> (= <code>.snapshot</code> / <code>.snap</code>)</td><td>C/E/K read</td><td>the live machine IS its snapshot: the whole self-model as one JSON <code>{chain,ip,k,e,lvl,levels,step,posix_code,exit_code}</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>${__vm__.stack}</code> (= <code>.kontinuation</code>) / <code>${__vm__.ip}</code> (= <code>.instruction_pointer</code> / <code>.control</code>)</td><td>K / C read</td><td>the saved frames (K) / the innermost goal (C), projected from <code>${__vm__}</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>${__vm__.frontier}</code></td><td>K read</td><td>the untried alternatives of the nearest choice frame, as a goal-list (the homoiconic backtracking frontier)</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>$(__ip__)</code> / <code>$(__step__)</code> / <code>$(__posix_code__)</code> / …</td><td>C read</td><td>the scheduler's seven registers: the cheap always-on reflection layer the snapshot reads from</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

So the machine is both **reflective** (read its own state) and **steerable** (rewrite its own future):
the `xfer` operators above don't just inspect the continuation, they *change* it and re-dispatch.

#### Reflective environment
<hr class="section-rule lvl-4">

By default E is explicit (`setenv`/`getenv`).  A **reflective** environment makes E reflect the
live shell env instead, so a goal uses plain `export <var>` and reads it first-class.

With reflection enabled, **context receive** re-hydrates E at a goal's entry (the dual of the automatic
**context send** on every transfer), so the body never calls `setenv`/`getenv`.  Three ways to receive,
same single source (`vm.ctx.receive`):

* the **`vm_hydrate` pragma** (`# cmk_pragma ::: { "vm_hydrate": true } :::`) makes the compiler
  auto-receive at *every* recipe entry, no annotation (the stage lives in this plugin; core lifts it
  when the pragma is set).  This is the recommended form;
* the **`@vm.ctx.hydrate` decorator**: per-goal opt-in (the eval-wrapping manual form);
* the bare `vm.ctx.hydrate` macro: for plain Makefiles (no compiler).

Receive is idempotent and `[ -s ]`-guarded (a no-op, no `jq`, when there is no context), and logs
`receiving context from <caller>` when it actually injects bindings.  Reflection is **dormant** (zero
cost, nothing created) unless declared.

#### Coroutines
<hr class="section-rule lvl-4">

The `vm-coroutines.cmk` demo packages the common pattern (a self-re-entering goal that advances
a `phase` through the reflective env until done) as a reusable, importable coroutine.  It lives under
`demos/` (not a library: no demos in libraries), so another demo imports it FROM there:

```make
$(call include.plugins, vm-coroutines.cmk prefix=demos/cmk)
__main__: coroutines.demo
```

Two knobs let a consumer tune it (e.g. an overlay/TUI): `coroutines.wait` (per-phase `io.wait` pacing)
and `coroutines.dump_env` (print the persisted reflective env when finished).

```bash {.cli_example}
$ ./demos/cmk/vm-coroutines.cmk
```

{{site.embed_demo('demos/cmk/vm-coroutines.cmk', type='cmk', github=github)}}

For coroutines in context alongside the other flow-control idioms (events, channels, typed exceptions),
see the [Flow Control]({{_}}/demos/flow-control/#coroutines) walkthrough.

The pending goals *are* the control stack, and a `goto`-loop runs **flat**; `MAKELEVEL` stays
constant, so the loop never nests processes:

```bash {.cli_example}
# the pending goals A,B after `peek` ARE the control stack:
$ ./demos/call_stack.mk peek A B
top=A rest=[B] depth=2

# a goto-loop that runs FLAT -- MAKELEVEL is constant, the loop does not nest processes:
$ ./demos/vm.mk count/3
count 3 (MAKELEVEL=3)
count 2 (MAKELEVEL=3)
count 1 (MAKELEVEL=3)
count 0 (MAKELEVEL=3)
done
```

#### Self-model & steering
<hr class="section-rule lvl-4">

{{ macros.protocol('__vm__',
    capability='The CEK virtual machine as a reflective, steerable self-model.',
    surface='`${make} __vm__.snapshot` emits the whole model as JSON; each name (`.stack` / `.ip` / `.backtrace` / ...) is both a macro (embed) and a same-name target (display).',
    notes='It can read its own state (chain, ip, stack depth, env, scheduler registers) and rewrite its own future (the goal frontier).',
    mkdocs=mkdocs) }}

The `__vm__.snapshot` target emits the **whole self-model** as one JSON object: `chain` (the K-frame
goals plus the top frame's pending continuation), `ip` (the innermost goal, the instruction pointer),
`k` (stack depth), `e` (the env), `lvl`/`levels` (the live make-level callstack), and
`step`/`posix_code`/`exit_code` (the scheduler registers, below).  Every observer reads it; the REPL
mode-line and the `overlay.mk` panel both render it.  It always emits **legal JSON**: when no machine
is live it degrades to `{}`, never an empty string.

The scheduler's seven registers are the **cheap always-on** layer of the same self-model: `__ip__`
(C), `__step__`/`__step_budget__`, `__alt__`/`__yielded__`, and `__posix_code__` vs
`__exit_code__` (raw wait status vs resolved code).  They are exported on every dispatch hop and
read with a bare make-var reference (no `jq`), defaulted empty so an unsupervised read is safe; the
snapshot single-sources `ip`/`step`/the codes from them and is the structured, opt-in view.  See the
[scheduler registers]({{_}}/runtime#flat-dispatch) on the Runtime page.

Each self-model name is both a **macro** and a **target** (no `.show` suffix): the `__vm__.stack` macro *embeds*
the value inside a recipe, while the `__vm__.stack` target *displays* it; the same var/target duality the
[program self-models]({{_}}/plugins/overview#program-self-models) share.  The JSON
observers (`.snapshot`, `.stack`, `.kontinuation`, `.frames`) emit legal JSON (`{}` / `[]` when idle);
the scalar projections (`.ip` / `.control`) are raw (the bare goal name, or nothing), and `.backtrace`
prints a human-readable tree.

#### Real frames &amp; the `vm_trace` pragma
<hr class="section-rule lvl-4">

Beyond the explicit K-stack, the `__vm__.frame.enter` macro turns a recipe's **shell** into a live frame:
prepended to a recipe it records the goal (the current target), a monotonic step, and make-level, installs a DEBUG trap
capturing the frame's most-recent shell command, and an EXIT trap recording the exit code + the
`__locals__` delta before popping.  Because a plain recursive sub-target nests a real child process, the set of
live frame files **is** the genuine deep call stack (the `__vm__.frames` target reads it), including
recursive `make` the K-stack can't see.

You rarely write `frame.enter` by hand: the **`vm_trace`** compile pragma makes the compiler's
[recipe-body joiner]({{_}}/cmk/compiler/#recipe-body-joining) **inject** it as the
first line of every eligible recipe (the same mechanism as the `target_locals` baseline).  It skips
infra/observer + private targets (`CMK_VM_TRACE_SKIP`) and recipes that *read* the VM, so the tracer
never traces itself.  `demos/cmk/debugger-example.cmk` enables it with one pragma key and ships zero
hand-placed calls; the whole program's call stack becomes the live frame-tree REPL.

```
# cmk_pragma ::: { "repl": { ... }, "vm_trace": true } :::
```

Because the transfer operators **steer** the running machine, a machine that *blocks for input* can be
driven from outside: `demos/cmk/vm-steer.cmk` is a REPL whose mode-line is the live dashboard and whose
prompt is the driver's seat: type a goal and the paused machine `vm.goto`s to it, runs it, and
re-blocks (the race-free `__vm__.steer.wait`/`__vm__.steer.post` command channel).

```bash {.cli_example}
$ ./demos/cmk/vm-steer.cmk            # then type a goal at the prompt, e.g. `vm-steer.alt`
```

{{site.embed_demo('demos/cmk/vm-steer.cmk', type='cmk', github=github)}}

